✓ PAYMENT RECOVERED

₹4,999 recovered
```

Then below:

### Audit Timeline

```text
19:42:01  Transaction detected
19:42:02  Risk evaluated
19:42:03  Agent diagnosis completed
19:42:03  Recovery strategy selected
19:42:03  Guardrail approved action
19:42:04  Payment link generated
19:42:08  Payment successful
19:42:08  Revenue recovered
```

---

# 🟠 P1: Add an "Agent Decision" panel

This should clearly distinguish:

### AI decision

> Retry payment.

from:

### System enforcement

> Retry permitted because this transaction has no previous retry within cooldown window.

This demonstrates your architecture.

---

# 🟠 P1: Add a simulation/control panel

For the demo, give yourself the ability to trigger:

* Payment success
* Payment failure
* Retry failure
* Retry limit reached
* High-value escalation
* Checkout abandonment
* Subscription failure

This is **very useful**.

You don't want to depend on randomness during a five-minute judging session.

A tiny:

> **Simulate Failure**

button can save your life.

---

# 🟡 P2: Add a "Why RecoverAI?" section

Somewhere in the dashboard:

### Traditional automation

```text
IF payment_failed
→ send reminder
```

### RecoverAI

```text
Detect
→ Diagnose
→ Select intervention
→ Check policy
→ Act
→ Verify
→ Learn from outcome
```

That makes the distinction extremely obvious.

---

# 🟡 P2: Add recovery strategy analytics

Dashboard:

```text
RECOVERY STRATEGIES

Payment Retry       68% success
Payment Link        54% success
Reminder            31% success
Discount            47% success
Human Escalation    —
```

This gives you another measurable dimension.

And now you can say:

> “The agent doesn't use one fixed recovery strategy. It selects an intervention based on transaction context.”

That's a much better story.

---

# 🟡 P2: Track agent performance

Add:

### Agent Metrics

* Cases processed
* Decisions made
* Actions executed
* Actions blocked
* Recovery success rate
* Escalation rate
* Average decision time

Potentially:

> **Autonomous Resolution Rate**

```text
Recovered automatically
─────────────────────────
Actionable cases
```

That is a great metric for an agentic system.

---

# 🟡 P2: Add a simple ROI calculation

This would be very nice.

Show:

```text
Revenue recovered       ₹1,17,200
Estimated manual cost   ₹18,000
Net recovered value     ₹99,200
```

Don't fabricate real-world benchmarks, though. If you're estimating operational savings, label the assumptions clearly.

---

# 🚫 Things I would NOT ask Antigravity to change

Do **not** tell it to:

* rewrite the backend
* replace SQLite
* replace React
* add LangChain just because "agents"
* add 15 more agents
* make every decision LLM-based
* add unnecessary authentication
* add a giant analytics system
* redesign the entire UI
* introduce microservices
* connect real customer/payment data

Your current architecture is perfectly adequate for the hackathon.

---

# 📋 The instruction I'd actually give Antigravity

You can give it this as the implementation brief:

> **Upgrade the existing RecoverAI project without rewriting or replacing the current architecture. Preserve the existing Node.js/Express backend, SQLite database, Claude tool-calling loop, SSE streaming, Razorpay integration, React/Vite/Tailwind frontend, existing routes, guardrails, seeded data, and working demo flow.**
>
> **The goal is to strengthen RecoverAI for Razorpay Track 03: AI Revenue Recovery.**
>
> **Required changes:**
>
> 1. Reframe the product workflow as **Detect → Diagnose → Decide → Guardrail → Act → Verify → Recover/Escalate**.
> 2. Support at least three revenue-risk scenarios: **failed payment, checkout abandonment, and subscription renewal failure**. Keep the current failed-payment workflow intact.
> 3. Make the agent's concise decision rationale visible in Case Detail. Do not expose chain-of-thought. Show diagnosis, recommended action, confidence, and relevant factors.
> 4. Strengthen the dashboard with database-derived metrics: total revenue at risk, revenue recovered, recovery rate, cases processed, recovered, failed, escalated, and guardrail-blocked actions.
> 5. Change the recovery funnel to show both transaction counts and monetary value through the stages: revenue at risk → actionable → action attempted → recovered → unresolved/escalated.
> 6. Strengthen and visibly enforce guardrails: maximum retries, cooldown period, maximum discount of 10%, high-value transaction escalation threshold, and maximum customer contact attempts. Do not allow the LLM to bypass these policies.
> 7. Add a visible escalation workflow for cases that exceed autonomous recovery limits.
> 8. Upgrade the Batch Run UI so each case visibly progresses through detected, diagnosing, decision, guardrail, action, and verified states. Final metrics must be calculated from actual database results, not hardcoded.
> 9. Upgrade Case Detail to show transaction amount, risk level, diagnosis, agent decision, guardrail result, recovery action, outcome, recovered amount, and a timestamped audit timeline.
> 10. Add a small deterministic demo/simulation control so specific outcomes can be triggered reliably during judging, including successful recovery, failed retry, retry-limit reached, and high-value escalation.
> 11. Add recovery-strategy analytics showing success rates for retry, payment link, reminder, discount, and escalation where applicable.
> 12. Add an **Autonomous Resolution Rate** metric and agent performance metrics.
> 13. Add a small ROI/recovered-value section, but clearly label any operational-cost figures as assumptions rather than real-world claims.
> 14. Keep the existing cinematic dark UI and improve it rather than replacing it.
> 15. Do not add unnecessary frameworks, microservices, authentication, or unrelated features.
> 16. After implementation, verify that the existing batch SSE flow, database persistence, guardrails, Razorpay test/simulation flow, dashboard, and case detail pages still work.
>
> **Most important:** this should remain a working end-to-end revenue recovery agent, not become a static dashboard. Every displayed metric should come from actual application state or clearly labeled simulation data.

### Priority

**P0:** 1–7
**P1:** 8–10
**P2:** 11–13

If Antigravity gets overwhelmed, **stop after P0**. That alone would make the project considerably stronger.

And one more thing: **don't let it generate fake "AI confidence" numbers just because the UI wants a percentage.** If confidence isn't actually produced by the model or a defined scoring mechanism, label it as a risk score or remove it. Judges who know what they're looking at can smell decorative AI from several kilometers away.
